<section class="section blog-list" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Blog">
<article class="blog-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/blog.html#routine-work"><p class="entry-index">LEADERSHIP / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h2 id="routine-work" itemprop="headline">AI is for routine work. People need time to think.</h2><div class="article-body" itemprop="articleBody"><p>The useful question about AI is not how many tasks a team can automate. It is what people could do with the time and attention that routine work consumes.</p><p>Start by watching the work. Which steps are repetitive? Where does a person interpret context or make a judgment? Which exceptions need an experienced owner? That map helps distinguish a task that can be assisted from a decision that needs a person.</p><p>Then trial a bounded change with the people who do the work. Measure the starting process and the changed one. Ask whether the new path reduced friction without making review harder or moving the burden somewhere else. Keep the person accountable for the judgment. If the evidence does not support the change, revise it or stop.</p></div></article>
<article class="blog-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/blog.html#make-accountability-real"><p class="entry-index">OPERATING PRACTICE / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h2 id="make-accountability-real" itemprop="headline">Make human oversight a working part of the system.</h2><div class="article-body" itemprop="articleBody"><p>A label such as “human in the loop” does not tell a team what to do. Oversight becomes real when a workflow identifies the person who reviews an exception, the evidence they need, the decisions they may make, and the path to follow when the system is uncertain or unavailable.</p><p>Those details should be designed with the people who will use the system. They need enough context to catch a poor result, a clear way to correct it, and a way to raise a concern without becoming a bottleneck for every routine case.</p><p>Leaders can inspect whether the handoff works by reviewing exceptions, overrides, and unresolved cases. That makes accountability visible in everyday operations and helps teams change controls when the work changes.</p></div></article>
<article class="blog-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/blog.html#evidence-before-scale"><p class="entry-index">MEASUREMENT / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h2 id="evidence-before-scale" itemprop="headline">Agree on evidence before a pilot begins.</h2><div class="article-body" itemprop="articleBody"><p>A pilot is more useful when the team decides in advance what would count as improvement. Choose measures that reflect the work: elapsed time, rework, exception rates, service quality, or user confidence. A single metric rarely captures the full effect.</p><p>Record the starting condition and include the people who understand the current process. After the change, compare like with like and write down limitations. A faster step may create more review elsewhere; a time estimate is not a financial return until the baseline and conversion are confirmed.</p><p>Use the evidence to make a decision: keep learning, adapt the approach, expand carefully, or stop. Being explicit about what a pilot did not establish makes the next decision more credible.</p></div></article>
</section>
